---
import { BUSINESS, REPOSITORY_URL } from '../config.ts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { absolute, breadcrumbs, ORGANIZATION, organization } from '../lib/structured.ts';

const mail = `mailto:${BUSINESS.email}`;
const structured = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: 'About SolLock',
    url: absolute('/about'),
    inLanguage: 'en',
    mainEntity: { '@id': ORGANIZATION['@id'] },
  },
  organization(),
  breadcrumbs([{ name: 'About' }]),
];
---

<Base
  title="About SolLock: who runs it, how it earns, how to check it"
  description="Who runs SolLock, how it makes money, where your Solana and your keys are while it is locked, and how to check all of it yourself."
  structured={structured}
>
  <h1 class="section-title">About SolLock</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    SolLock is a small tool with one job: it locks your own Solana until a date you pick, in a way nobody can undo, its maker included. This page
    says who is behind it, how it earns its keep, and how to check what it does.
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>
      A website that builds one Solana transaction for you. The transaction puts your Solana in an account with a built-in time lock, which is a
      feature of the Solana network itself. You read the transaction and sign it in your own wallet, and from then on the network keeps the lock.
      SolLock has been open on Solana's main network since 2 November 2026.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a commitment device, not an investment. Locked Solana earns nothing, and it keeps following the price of Solana, which can go down as
      well as up.
    </p>

    <h2>Who runs it</h2>
    <p>
      SolLock is the trade name of a sole trader in {BUSINESS.place}{BUSINESS.kvk && `, registered with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK) under number ${BUSINESS.kvk}`}: a one-person business. One person builds and runs the site, answers for what the guides say, and reads the mail at <a href={mail}>{BUSINESS.email}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How it earns</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A fee for each lock.</strong> 0.25% of the amount you lock, at least 0.01 SOL, VAT included. It is paid in the same transaction that
        makes the lock, and the page shows it before you sign. Test locks are free.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Affiliate links.</strong> The lock page lists exchanges where you can buy Solana. If you sign up through one of those links, the
        exchange may pay SolLock a commission, and your price stays the same. The links are marked as what they are: see{' '}
        <a href="/affiliates">affiliate links</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>That is all of it. There is no advertising, no token and no data to sell.</p>

    <h2>What stays in your hands</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your Solana and your keys.</strong> The transaction is built in your browser and signed in your wallet. The server signs nothing and
        holds nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The date.</strong> A lock opens on its date and not before, for anyone. The one key that could open it early does not exist: see{' '}
        <a href="/custodian">the custodian address</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The way back in.</strong> Your wallet's recovery phrase is the only thing that opens a lock on its date, so nobody, SolLock included,
        can replace a lost one. Back it up before you lock anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your data.</strong> SolLock has no accounts, no database and no analytics. The <a href="/privacy">privacy statement</a> says exactly what
        passes through its server.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to check it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Read the code.</strong> The site is source-available: the code that builds your lock is public on <a href={REPOSITORY_URL}>GitHub</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read the transaction.</strong> Before your wallet opens, the lock page shows what you are about to sign in plain words, and every
        instruction of it under "The full transaction".
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the custodian.</strong> <a href="/custodian">The custodian page</a> shows how the address is made and lets you redo the calculation
        yourself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check a lock on the blockchain.</strong>{' '}
        <a href="/learn/verify-solana-lock-on-chain">How to check a Solana lock on the blockchain</a> goes through it field by field.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Try it first.</strong> A <a href="/lock?test">free test lock</a> of 0.01 SOL opens again after about a day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Do it without SolLock.</strong> Solana's own command-line tool makes the same lock:{' '}
        <a href="/learn/solana-cli-stake-account-lockup">How to make a locked stake account with the Solana command line</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Found a security problem? Please report it privately, through the Security tab of the <a href={REPOSITORY_URL}>repository</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How the guides are written</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/learn">guides</a> are written in plain words, for people new to crypto and for people who want the details. Facts are checked
      against Solana's own documentation and against the network itself. Each guide carries the date it was written or last changed, and none of
      them says what a price is going to do. They are general information, not financial, legal or tax advice. If you find a mistake, write to{' '}
      <a href={mail}>{BUSINESS.email}</a> and it gets fixed.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
